Not yet.The pldmd side is a local/downstream integration at the moment, so I don't have public patches to point at. I provide a small consumer example here.

1.
pldmd parses the Redfish
Resource PDRs per device (tid) into a binding map, caches it by tid, and passes
the matching map to decode()/encode(); libbej itself is tid-agnostic and only
substitutes placeholders.
2.
// Built once per device from its PDRs, cached as perDevice[tid].
BejDeferredBindingMap bindings;
bindings[bejBinding::resourceLink(30)] =
"/redfish/v1/Systems/1/Storage/1/Drives/1"; // %L30
bindings[bejBinding::instanceId(30)] = "1"; // %I30
bindings[bejBinding::action(30, 1)] =
"/redfish/v1/Systems/1/Storage/1/Drives/1/Actions/Drive.Reset"; // %T30.1
bindings[bejBinding::chassis()] = "/redfish/v1/Chassis/1U"; // %C (no id)

3.
// Decode a device response into client-facing JSON (placeholders resolved):
decoder.decode(dictionaries, encodedResponse, bindings);

// Encode a request to the device (values rewritten back to placeholders):
encoder.encode(&dictionaries, schemaClass, requestTree, bindings);

I think there is no point calling `addDeferredBindingString` if `length <= 0`
```
if (length > 0)
{
  if (deferredBinding)
  {
      addDeferredBindingString(params, value, length);
  } else {
      params->output->append(value, length - 1);
  }
}
```
